,"what_it_is":"Humulene is a volatile organic compound, specifically a monocyclic sesquiterpene, that gives many plants their earthy, woody, and spicy aromas. You likely encounter it every day in the scent of blooming flowers, the distinct flavor of hoppy beers, and the rich smell of culinary herbs.","found_in":"It is famously found in hops (Humulus lupulus), which gives the molecule its name, as well as in rosemary, bitter orange, and shampoo ginger.","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are drawn to humulene for its potential as a bioactive phytochemical, particularly exploring its role in inflammation and pain pathways. While the current standardized evidence level is graded as none found, the sheer volume of over 900 papers—including recent structural studies on how these molecules might inhibit pain receptors—shows strong scientific momentum. Scientists are eager to understand exactly how this common botanical compound interacts with the human body.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"anti-inflammatory","statement":"Early literature points toward humulene as a compound of interest for supporting healthy inflammatory responses.","evidence_level":"none_found","basis":"This rests on 12 papers, including preliminary human and animal research.","eco":null,"oxford":null,"evidence_lines":[],"paper_count":12},{"claim_id":"pain","statement":"Researchers are exploring whether this terpene may support pain management.","evidence_level":"none_found","basis":"This is based on 3 papers, including recent structural models of receptor inhibition.","eco":null,"oxford":null,"evidence_lines":[],"paper_count":3},{"claim_id":"antioxidant","statement":"Humulene is being studied for its potential to support cellular health against oxidative stress.","evidence_level":"none_found","basis":"This rests on 7 papers spanning preliminary human, animal, and review designs.","eco":null,"oxford":null,"evidence_lines":[],"paper_count":7},{"claim_id":"insect","statement":"Early work points toward humulene having properties that may help repel insects.","evidence_level":"none_found","basis":"This is supported by 6 papers, primarily from animal and observational models.","eco":null,"oxford":null,"evidence_lines":[],"paper_count":6}],"hypothesized_benefits":[{"claim_id":"cancer","area":"Oncology research","hypothesis":"Researchers are exploring whether humulene might influence cellular pathways related to tumor growth, and if this bears out, it could become a valuable phytochemical in cancer research.","evidence_level":"none_found","paper_count":11,"study_to_test_it":"A controlled in vivo animal study measuring tumor progression and cellular markers over several weeks to establish a baseline for potential human trials.","eco":null,"oxford":null,"evidence_lines":[]},{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","area":"Microbial defense","hypothesis":"There is open interest in whether humulene can help combat microbial threats, which could make it a useful natural additive for food preservation or topical applications.","evidence_level":"none_found","paper_count":11,"study_to_test_it":"Standardized in vitro minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) assays against common bacterial and fungal strains to quantify its direct antimicrobial efficacy.","eco":null,"oxford":null,"evidence_lines":[]},{"claim_id":"anxiety","area":"Mood and relaxation","hypothesis":"Early animal work points toward a potential role in promoting calm, raising the question of whether humulene contributes to the relaxing effects of the plants that contain it.","evidence_level":"none_found","paper_count":1,"study_to_test_it":"A behavioral animal study using standardized anxiety models, such as the elevated plus maze, to observe the effects of isolated humulene administration.","eco":null,"oxford":null,"evidence_lines":[]},{"claim_id":"neuroprotection","area":"Brain health","hypothesis":"Scientists are curious if humulene's properties might help protect neural tissues from damage or age-related decline.","evidence_level":"none_found","paper_count":1,"study_to_test_it":"An in vivo animal model of neurodegeneration measuring cognitive performance and brain tissue biomarkers over several months.","eco":null,"oxford":null,"evidence_lines":[]}],"associated_conditions":[{"condition":"Ulcerative colitis","mondo_id":null,"relationship":"detected in metabolomic studies of"},{"condition":"Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","mondo_id":null,"relationship":"detected in metabolomic studies of"}],"cautions":null,"evidence_at_a_glance":"While humulene is the subject of over 900 scientific papers spanning animal, in vitro, and early human studies, standardized clinical evidence reviews currently grade the definitive evidence level as none found, highlighting that research is still in the exploratory phase."}}
